{
 "phase": "W3a: frame time and memory of the browser host",
 "change": "load_memory.py --web BUILD_DIR (the browser host through web_run.mjs in NTSD_BROWSER_ENGINE's engine) and --wasi BUILD_DIR (the headless build under Node's WASI through wasi_run.mjs): the Mac run's arguments (the scenario's app_e2e script, virtual clock 123456789 8, music, sounds and network off) and events, reporting the game's own clocks only: loadSeconds, startedToLoadedSeconds ('started' to 'loaded' by the game's uptime, added to the Mac runs too), completeMsPerTick (busy time per tick between the progress events at bodies A and B), ticksPerSecond, windowCycles, exitCode; builds as (kind, path); --ticks multiples of 300. W2b-4c's queued page/worker items: the slash redirect stops the script (hash kept), ntsdPage.exit on a failed start, pauses before read retries. No game, Core, Runtime or Swift change.",
 "how": "Per scenario and host, 3 runs: vs over bodies 600–1500, the Demo over 300–900 (--bodies 300); Chromium 153 headless shell and Playwright's WebKit with build-wasm-web's NTSDWeb.wasm, Node 26 WASI with build-wasm's NTSDHeadless.wasm, macOS with the frozen unchecked NTSDHeadless of the tw-w2b3 gate; one host at a time on this Mac",
 "comparison": "completeMsPerTick on the browser and WASI is wall time on the one game thread with the frame's rendering inline (WASI has no render thread). On the Mac by default it is the game thread only, its pixel work running on the render thread alongside, and processMsPerTick is CPU summed over all threads (thread hops and queues included) — neither is like for like. The like-for-like Mac figure is 'macsync': the same binary with --synchronous-render (load_memory.py --game-arg), rendering on its game thread as WASI does. ticksPerSecond is mostly the virtual clock's pacing, not compute",
